It is required that the hospice discharge summary provided to a facility receiving a hospice patient for care—or to the patient’s community attending physician upon hospice discharge—includes at least the following: 
1. A summary of the patient’s hospice stay, including treatments, symptoms, and pain management; 
2. The patient’s current plan of care; 
3. The patient’s latest physician orders; 
4. Any other documentation that will assist in the post-discharge continuity of care or that is requested by the receiving facility or the attending physician.

This standard was not met as evidenced by clinical record review and interview. In 1 of 1 (100%) clinical record reviewed of a patient that transferred to a hospital, it was revealed that the hospice did not ensure that a discharge summary was completed to include a summary of the patient's hospice stay that documented treatments, symptom management, pain management, the plan of care, latest physician orders, and any other documentation that would assist in the post-discharge continuity of care. 

CLINICAL RECORD 3: The patient transferred to a hospital on 11/08/2025 to seek aggressive treatment. Documentation that the patient's medication list, history and physical and discharge summary had not been forwarded to the receiving facility. There was no discharge summary completed.

During an interview and joint review of clinical records on 01/15/2026 with the Director of Clinical Services, it was confirmed that the discharge summary was not documented with the required elements.

