Review of the CDCs (Center for Disease Control) Clean Hands Count for Healthcare Providers revealed hand hygiene should be used when: Multiple opportunities for hand hygiene may occur during a single care episode. Following are the clinical indications for hand hygiene: immediately before touching a patient; before performing an aseptic task or handling invasive medical devices; before moving from work on a soiled body site to a clean body site on the same patient; after touching a patient or the patients immediate environment; after contact with blood, body fluids or contaminated surfaces; and immediately after glove removal. Further review revealed wear gloves, according to Standard Precautions, when it can be reasonably anticipated that contact with blood or other potentially infectious materials, mucous membranes, non-intact skin, potentially contaminated skin or contaminated equipment could occur. Review revelaed gloves are not a substitute for hand hygiene; if your task requires gloves, perform hand hygiene prior to donning gloves, before touching the patient or the patient environment; perform hand hygiene immediately after removing gloves;

