Review of the agency's policy titled, "Advance Directives," revised 04/01/2021, showed, in part:
-The agency complies with all State and Federal laws regarding advance directives and informs and distributes written information to the patient on his or her right to formulate advance directives; 
- During the admission interview, and prior to receiving care, the hospice nurse or social worker provides written information and instructions on advance directives to the patient. If the patient is unable to understand this information it is given to the patients legal health care representative or proxy; 
 - In the administrative section of the patients clinical record, the hospice nurse or social worker documents that the patient has received written information related to advance directives and whether the patient has or has not executed an advance directive; 
- Education is provided to hospice staff and the community regarding advance directives, advance care planning and patient rights regarding advance directives; and 
 - The agency recognizes all patients have a fundamental right to make decisions relating to their own medical treatment, including the right to accept or refuse medical treatment. Valid advance directives will be followed to the extent permitted and required by law. 


